{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aab243a2519c1e25","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020 (AMD GPU / ROCm enablement for serving and training)\n11\t\n12\tTriage result: **not triaged** — the strategy spans four components and two owning\n13\tteams, includes a P0 hardware validation campaign, and contains unresolved\n14\texternal dependencies; neither the below-threshold nor the docs-only check\n15\tapplies.\n16\t\n17\t## Epic List\n18\t\n19\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n20\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign — vLLM ROCm serving functional and performance parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for validated models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | PyTorch ROCm training support for single- and multi-GPU PyTorchJob workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling for ROCm workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Document AMD GPU (ROCm) supported hardware, drivers, and validated models | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | P1 |\n26\t\n27\t## Dependency DAG\n28\t\n29\t```mermaid\n30\tgraph TD\n31\t    E001[\"E001 ROCm serving validation campaign (Investigation, P0)\"]\n32\t    E002[\"E002 vLLM ROCm serving runtime productization (P0)\"]\n33\t    E003[\"E003 PyTorch ROCm training support (P1)\"]\n34\t    E004[\"E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling (P1)\"]\n35\t    E005[\"E005 AMD ROCm documentation (docs-authoring, P1)\"]\n36\t    E001 --> E002\n37\t    E001 --> E005\n38\t    E002 --> E005\n39\t    E003 --> E005\n40\t    E004 --> E005\n41\t```\n42\t\n43\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E005 (length 3).\n44\t\n45\t## DAG Justification\n46\t\n47\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n48\t|------|------|-----------|\n49\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | HLR-2 productizes the runtime \"for the models the validation campaign clears\" — the campaign's outcome determines the productization epic's scope (and existence, on a full no-go). True gate: `rewrite` (scope rewrites to the cleared subset); `remove` if no model clears. |\n50\t| E001 → E005 | Content dependency | HLR-4 requires docs to state the validated model list — a direct deliverable of the campaign, not only of productization. |\n51\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs describe the productized serving runtime and its supported model list. |\n52\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs describe what was built (training support). |\n53\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs describe what was built (quota handling). |\n54\t| no edge E001 ↔ E003, E001 ↔ E004 | Rule 14 / parallel tracks | Serving validation and PyTorch training support are independent HLRs in different repos/components; neither builds against the other's artifacts. Shared AMD hardware is a coordination/scheduling risk, not a structural dependency. |\n55\t| no edge E003 ↔ E004 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifact) | Training Operator job support (opendatahub-io/training-operator) and Kueue amd.com/gpu quota configuration (Distributed Workloads config) are separate codebases with no shared artifact. The end-to-end HLR-3 capability (ROCm PyTorchJob admitted through Kueue) is divided per slice: job-level execution ACs on the training epic, quota/admission ACs on the Kueue epic; the same team (Distributed Workloads) integration-tests both. |\n56\t\n57\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n58\t\n59\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n60\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n61\t| HLR-1 — validation campaign producing parity data and go/no-go scope | P0 | E001 | Investigation epic; produces the go/no-go scope that gates productization. |\n62\t| HLR-2 — supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for cleared models | P0 | E002 | Scope parametric on the campaign's cleared-model list. **KServe validation bundled as an AC** on E002: KServe is a passive component in this strategy (no code changes expected — the ROCm runtime is deployed through the existing ServingRuntime/InferenceService surfaces), and KServe shares E002's owning team (RHOAI Serving), so per the passive-component rule its validation is an acceptance criterion on the nearest active epic rather than a separate epic. Splitting would create an epic with no code deliverable. |\n63\t| HLR-3 — PyTorch training on ROCm via Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota | P1 | E003, E004 | One HLR spanning two canonical components with the same owning team; split per Epic Boundary Rule 1 (different component → separate epics) along the architectural seam: PyTorchJob/ROCm image support (Training Operator) vs. quota/admission configuration (Distributed Workloads). The lower-level split is not a priority split — both epics are P1 and run in parallel; the end-to-end admission capability is covered by per-slice ACs (see DAG Justification). |\n64\t| HLR-4 — documentation of supported hardware, drivers, validated models | P2 | E005 | `docs-authoring` epic; priority P1 derived from strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` per the docs-authoring priority exception (not the HLR's P2 label). |\n65\t\n66\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n67\t\n68\t## Health Warnings\n69\t\n70\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no formal Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Owner teams were derived from `.context/architecture-context/` owning-team fields (RHOAI Serving: KServe, Serving Runtimes; Distributed Workloads: Training Operator, Distributed Workloads). The Documentation epic's owner (\"RHOAI Docs\") is an assumption — confirm the actual docs owner before submission. Epic ACs were derived from the HLR text.\n71\t- **Availability precondition (open question 1 — hardware access)**: the validation campaign and both training epics need dedicated AMD Instinct nodes; procurement/partner-lab access is unresolved and externally controlled. Per the AC rules this is recorded as a risk on the affected epics (E001, E003, E004), not as an acceptance criterion — the ACs sit on the deliverables (the validated report, the completing training runs).\n72\t- **External cadence risk (open question 2 — upstream ROCm build cadence)**: AMD/upstream ROCm image releases may not align with our release trains. This does not change which epics exist or what they do (productization proceeds either way, pinning whatever ROCm image version validates), so it is not an Investigation epic — it is handled as a pinned-validated-image AC plus schedule risk on E002, and a Health Warning here.\n73\t- **KServe is passive**: the strategy lists KServe as affected, but no KServe code change is identified — the ROCm runtime deploys through existing KServe surfaces. If implementation reveals KServe changes are actually needed (e.g. accelerator discovery or scheduling changes), raise a new KServe epic rather than expanding E002.\n74\t- **Docs priority**: E005 carries P1 (strategy priority Major → P1 per the docs-authoring exception) although HLR-4 is labeled P2. This is rule-driven, not an inversion.\n75\t- **Shared hardware contention**: the serving campaign (E001) and training validation (E003/E004) draw on the same scarce AMD Instinct hardware; sequencing between them is a scheduling decision, not a DAG dependency.\n76\t\n77\t## Tiered Delivery\n78\t\n79\tNot applicable: no upstream PR/RFC contribution epic exists (the strategy consumes\n80\tpublished upstream ROCm images rather than contributing upstream), so the\n81\tRule 16/17 external-dependency tiering does not apply. Partial value arrives\n82\tnaturally: the campaign (E001) and training support (E003/E004) deliver\n83\tindependently of the productization epic (E002).\n84\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9936055345aec342","agent_id":"a4d491859d5c726cb","session_id":"83c98df4-c9fc-48fe-b897-027eac3b0312","uuid":"496066ce-d705-4c41-8f92-484ca2ec98b0","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:29:23.991Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aab243a2519c1e25","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
