{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a12bfe1cc756e675","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AI Signal Rationale — RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 (Implementation)\n2\t\n3\tProductize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for validated models.\n4\t\n5\t| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n6\t|--------|-------|-----------|\n7\t| change_specificity | 0 | The target repo (midstream vLLM) and the deliverable shape (runtime image + ServingRuntime packaging) are identifiable, but the strategy states no file paths or build specifics, and the scope (cleared model list) is parametric on the campaign outcome. |\n8\t| pattern_precedent | 1 | The same codebase already productizes CUDA vLLM runtime builds per platform version; adding a ROCm variant follows an established build/release pattern (upstream ROCm builds also exist as reference). |\n9\t| adapter_pattern | 1 | The existing CUDA vLLM runtime productization (image build, ServingRuntime packaging, release cadence) is the reference implementation this epic mirrors for ROCm. |\n10\t| existing_foundation | 1 | Extends the existing midstream vLLM repo, its build pipeline, and the existing ServingRuntime packaging — no greenfield surface. |\n11\t| open_questions | -1 | The epic's scope is explicitly gated on the validation campaign's go/no-go outcome (which models clear), an open question that changes what gets productized. |\n12\t| external_dependency | -1 | Depends on upstream ROCm runtime image releases whose cadence is controlled by AMD and the upstream communities and may not align with our release trains (strategy open question 2). |\n13\t| human_process_gates | 0 | No explicit human approval step is stated; the support declaration follows from the campaign's data-driven go/no-go scope. |\n14\t| repo_access | 1 | The midstream vLLM repo (opendatahub-io/vllm) is an accessible midstream repository. |\n15\t| architecture_claims | 1 | Corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.