. The Documentation epic's owner (\"RHOAI Docs\") is an assumption — confirm the actual docs owner before submission. Epic ACs were derived from the HLR text.\n70\t- **Availability precondition (open question 1 — hardware access)**: the validation campaign and both training epics need dedicated AMD Instinct nodes; procurement/partner-lab access is unresolved and externally controlled. Per the AC rules this is recorded as a risk on the affected epics (E001, E003, E004), not as an acceptance criterion — the ACs sit on the deliverables (the validated report, the completing training runs).\n71\t- **External cadence risk (open question 2 — upstream ROCm build cadence)**: AMD/upstream ROCm image releases may not align with our release trains. This does not change which epics exist or what they do (productization proceeds either way, pinning whatever ROCm image version validates), so it is not an Investigation epic — it is handled as a pinned-validated-image AC plus schedule risk on E002, and a Health Warning here.\n72\t- **KServe is passive**: the strategy lists KServe as affected, but no KServe code change is identified — the ROCm runtime deploys through existing KServe surfaces. If implementation reveals KServe changes are actually needed (e.g. accelerator discovery or scheduling changes), raise a new KServe epic rather than expanding E002.\n73\t- **Docs priority**: E005 carries P1 (strategy priority Major → P1 per the docs-authoring exception) although HLR-4 is labeled P2. This is rule-driven, not an inversion.\n74\t- **Shared hardware contention**: the serving campaign (E001) and training validation (E003/E004) draw on the same scarce AMD Instinct hardware; sequencing between them is a scheduling decision, not a DAG dependency.\n75\t\n76\t## Tiered Delivery\n77\t\n78\tNot applicable: no upstream PR/RFC contribution epic exists (the strategy consumes\n79\tpublished upstream ROCm images rather than contributing upstream), so the\n80\tRule 16/17 external-dependency tieri