{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-872a55f561672bf1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AI Signal Rationale — RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 (Investigation)\n2\t\n3\tAMD Instinct validation campaign — vLLM ROCm serving functional and performance parity.\n4\t\n5\t| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n6\t|--------|-------|-----------|\n7\t| question_specificity | 0 | The questions are concrete and enumerable (per-model functional pass/fail; performance vs the CUDA baseline for the supported model set), but the strategy does not define the pass/fail criterion for \"parity\" — the thresholds and measurement methodology must be framed as part of the campaign design itself. |\n8\t| source_accessibility | 0 | No readable artifact holds the answer: the platform has no first-hand ROCm parity data (the strategy states this explicitly), and upstream ROCm support docs do not answer parity for our supported model set. The answer must be produced by executing on hardware. |\n9\t| local_runnability | 0 | vLLM ROCm serving cannot be exercised as a downloaded binary or pip library without AMD Instinct GPUs; there is no embedded/bundled substitute for the physical accelerators the campaign exists to validate. |\n10\t| cluster_hardware_dependence | -2 | The gating question — functional and performance parity on AMD Instinct — requires physical AMD GPU hardware (dedicated nodes, likely multi-GPU for serving configurations); none exists in the CI fleet today. |\n11\t| human_judgment_required | -1 | The gating parity question is technical, but peripheral items need external-party input: hardware access via procurement or partner-lab coordination is unresolved and outside our control. |\n12\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8344a3e9309bc020","agent_id":"a8f683bbbe8fefecb","session_id":"83c98df4-c9fc-48fe-b897-027eac3b0312","uuid":"3455088a-86c3-4409-b813-88f2e9b9dd67","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:33:52.988Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-872